    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The invasion of California grasslands is striking and extensive: since the 19th century, invasive annual grasses from the Mediterranean have come to dominate 9.2 million hectares of grassland previously dominated by native perennial grasses. As in many species invasions, the mechanisms driving the spread of invasive species in these grasslands remain poorly understood. Disease, which may prevent and/or facilitate invasions through its influence on the outcomes of interspecies competition, represents a relatively unexplored but potentially powerful driver in this invasive event. California grassland plant communities found in serpentine soils with high heavy metal content and low essential plant nutrient availability are exceptional in that they remain largely uninvaded and continue to include diverse native grasses and forbs. In these remnant communities, I assessed the potential for foliar disease to play a role in maintaining and/or reducing native plant persistence in California grasslands. To do this, I: 1) measured foliar disease burden in serpentine and nonserpentine populations of the native bunchgrass Stipa pulchra, 2) compared the fungal foliar pathogen communities hosted by the surveyed plants, and 3) analyzed the chemical makeup of serpentine and nonserpentine plant tissue in order to understand potential impacts of soil chemistry on plant health and pathogen community composition. I found that serpentine and nonserpentine Stipa experience low disease pressure from distinct communities of foliar pathogens, and that serpentine plants, like the soil they grow in, have elevated Ni and Mg content and decreased C, N, Ca, and P content compared to nonserpentine plants. The results imply that pathogens are unlikely to affect the persistence of this native plant, but that plants may structure their pathogen communities via either plant community composition or via tissue chemistry. Plant invasions and/or soil type are shown to have potentially far-reaching effects on pathogen diversity and community composition, with implications for efforts to conserve native plant communities and to beneficially shift public perceptions of parasites.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
